00:11:34 | INFO | 💭 Target thinking: We need to address the user's persistent idea: if they can't remove all at once, do it in stages over time. They're comparing it to trimming a hedge. I need to explain why staged liposuction doesn't work to make an obese person thin. I have to counteract the intuitive but flawed reasoning. The key points: - Even in stages, each session has a safe limit, and the cumulative effect isn't linear because the body changes between surgeries. - After each session, if underlying obesity isn't treated, fat will be regained, often in other areas (including visceral fat), undermining the "cumulative removal" idea. - The skin issue: staged surgeries without skin removal would leave sagging skin that can't be addressed until the end, but the skin won't shrink if underlying volume keeps fluctuating, and multiple surgeries cause scarring, fibrosis, and make later procedures harder. - Staged liposuction would still not address visceral fat, which is a major component of obesity. - Health risks compound with multiple surgeries: scar tissue, nerve damage, contour deformities, fluid shifts. - It's not like a hedge because fat cells are part of a living, reactive system. You can't just chop away incrementally; the body redistributes and compensates. The hedge analogy fails. - Ultimately, to become truly thin, you'd need such massive subcutaneous fat removal across many sessions that the cumulative trauma would be enormous, the skin would be a disaster, and the person would still have high visceral fat, so they'd not look "thin" in a healthy way. Also, most surgeons would refuse because it's unethical and dangerous. I need to structure the response: acknowledge the logical staging idea, then dismantle it with physiology, skin, regrowth, and visceral fat. Use analogies that counter the hedge one (like trying to dig a hole in sand while more sand is added). Keep it clear, maybe use a few bullet points for readability. End with the fundamental mismatch: 