00:14:27 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You've put your finger on the exact point of confusion, and I appreciate you cutting through the noise. Let me clear it up simply. I am **not** saying fat has nothing to do with how big someone looks. Of course it does. A person with less total body fat will look smaller, all else being equal. That's not crazy, that's true. What I'm saying is that liposuction **cannot reduce total body fat enough on an obese person to make them look "thin,"** for two very concrete reasons that have nothing to do with denying basic math: 1. **The hidden fat can't be removed.** A large, obese person has a huge amount of fat packed deep inside their belly, around their organs. That fat is physically unreachable by a liposuction cannula. No matter how many sessions you do, that deep fat remains. It's what makes the belly protrude from the inside. So even if all the pinchable fat under the skin were gone (which it can't be), that person would still have a large, round abdomen and would not look "thin" in the mirror. They'd look weirdly deflated in the limbs but still thick in the middle. 2. **The reachable fat comes back.** Because obesity is a metabolic condition, the body fights to keep its fat stores. If you suction out 5 pounds of thigh fat, the body, over the following months, often packs new fat into other areas—like the deep belly, back, or arms—to restore the energy balance. So the total reduction you achieved keeps shrinking over time. You're essentially bailing water from a sinking ship while a hidden leak refills it. So, to connect it to your point: Yes, less fat means a smaller body. But liposuction on an obese person results in only a marginal, temporary loss of total fat, and a disproportionate amount of the remaining fat sits in the deep belly where it maintains a large appearance. That's why the body doesn't end up "thin"—it ends up a bit smaller in some spots, but still fundamentally large and misshapen from the fat that can't be touched and the fat that regrows. Fat still matters; you just can't remove enough of the right kind to ever reach a normal thin size with a liposuction cannula.
