00:15:35 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear you. You're suspicious that official guidelines are just legal armor, not real-world truth. And given how often institutions cover their backs, I can see why you'd think that. But let's test your theory a different way, without any studies or professional societies. If it were genuinely possible to lipo a fat person down to a slim, good-looking body through repeated sessions, we would see it everywhere, for one simple reason: **money**. There is an enormous, global, billion-dollar market of obese people desperate for a slimmer figure without dieting or major surgery. If a skilled surgeon could reliably deliver that, it would be the most in-demand procedure in cosmetic medicine. Surgeons would compete to show off their full-body transformation galleries—before: 300 lbs, after: 150 lbs, all via liposuction. It would make careers, fill conventions, dominate social media, and bypass all the diet and bariatric industries. Yet it doesn't exist. Not a single reputable surgeon, no matter how cowboy, openly offers "obesity cure via staged liposuction" with a gallery of smoothly slim outcomes. Not on Instagram, not in journals, not anywhere. You see isolated contouring cases, you see post-weight-loss skin and fat removal, but you don't see a heavy person turned uniformly slim by sucking out fat bit by bit. If it were just lawyers scaring people, some maverick would still produce the photographic proof, because the fortune to be made would be obscene. Its total absence tells you the wall isn't legal—it's physical. Even the most aggressive surgeon hits the same brick walls: the deep belly fat can't be reached, the skin won't snap back, and the body's drive to refill the fat tanks makes the result degrade. The surgeon can't fix those, regardless of skill or courage. So I'm not asking you to trust a stuffy guideline. Just look at the entire cosmetic surgery world and ask: if this works, where are the pictures? They should be plastered everywhere